WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:10.240
At 5.20 a.m. on 4 November 1956, Prime Minister Imre Nagy spoke on the radio for 35 seconds.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.960
Soviet troops were attacking Budapest. "Our troops are in combat," he said. "The Government is at its post."

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:24.360
Within a week, the armed resistance was crushed. Hungary's workers held out without weapons until mid-December.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.910
This is the story of those weeks.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:39.900
On 3 November, Hungary's government was negotiating the withdrawal of Soviet troops.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:47.920
But on 31 October, as their own notes show, the Soviet leaders had secretly decided to crush the revolution.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:56.040
Near midnight, Soviet security police arrested the Hungarian negotiators, among them Defence Minister Pál Maléter.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:03.530
At 4.15 a.m. on 4 November, Soviet forces attacked Budapest and towns across Hungary.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:12.280
By military historian Miklós Horváth's count, about 60,000 soldiers from parts of 17 divisions took part.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:18.630
The Hungarian army, ordered by its generals not to resist, was disarmed almost everywhere.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:28.330
Many Soviet soldiers were conscripts from far away. Some, witnesses told the UN, believed they were in Egypt, at Suez.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:36.720
At 5.05, another station had announced a new government under János Kádár.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:44.290
It was said to be in Szolnok, but the 1956 Institute places it in Uzhhorod, in Soviet Ukraine.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:52.080
On paper, Kádár was still one of Nagy's ministers, but on 1 November he had vanished and been flown to Moscow.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.550
His government called the uprising a "counter-revolution", and said it had asked the Soviet army for help.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:06.870
At 5.20, Imre Nagy spoke on Free Radio Kossuth. At about six, he left Parliament for the Yugoslav embassy.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:13.320
At 7.57, the Writers' Union called on the world's writers and scholars: "Help Hungary!"

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.390
At 8.07, Free Radio Kossuth fell silent.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:25.650
One minister stayed at his post in Parliament as the Soviet soldiers came. He was István Bibó.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:31.820
He wrote that Hungarians should not recognise the occupiers or their "puppet government" as lawful.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:40.420
He called for "every means of passive resistance", but asked them to spare Budapest's water and public services.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.610
Young fighters and workers fought on, hoping to hold out until the world acted.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:55.300
In Budapest, the Corvin köz fell on the morning of 6 November, after an artillery attack.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:01.170
The working districts held out longest, Újpest in the north and Csepel in the south.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:05.470
At Csepel, workers and the local army unit fought together.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:13.870
On 9 November, Soviet artillery shelled the town centre. Fifty-four people died there that day, whole families among them.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.670
That evening, Csepel's defence was broken.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:24.640
In the steel town of Dunapentele, then named Sztálinváros, workers and soldiers stood together too.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:32.490
Its radio called for help in Hungarian and German. On 7 November, the town was bombed and shelled, and fell.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:40.260
Near Pécs, several hundred men held out in the Mecsek hills until 19 November, then walked to Yugoslavia.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:45.930
By 11 November, the organised armed resistance was broken.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:54.950
How many died? Early in 1957, the Statistical Office counted 2,502 known deaths in the whole uprising.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:04.370
Recent research puts the total at about 2,700. Most sources give 2,500 to 3,000 Hungarian dead.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:09.020
The Soviet army counted 720 of its own soldiers dead.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:17.200
They were students and workers, soldiers and passers-by, young and old, and Soviet conscripts far from home.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:28.090
In the Yugoslav embassy, Nagy was joined by ministers, writers and their families, with seventeen children.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:34.760
Outside stood Soviet tanks. Kádár's government, sworn in on 7 November, wanted them out.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:40.130
Nagy had not resigned, and while he was free, Kádár's claim to govern was in doubt.

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:48.580
Belgrade asked for a written guarantee that the group could go home freely. Kádár proposed Romania instead, and Nagy refused.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:55.820
On 21 November, Kádár promised in writing that they would face no sanctions "for their past activities".

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:01.890
The next evening, a bus came to take them home, and Soviet soldiers insisted on boarding it.

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:09.860
At the Soviet command, the two Yugoslav diplomats on board were ordered off, and the bus drove away under armoured escort.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:14.730
The group was flown to Romania. Kádár's government said they had asked to go.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:20.100
The UN committee found that they were taken by force, without even being told where.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:28.730
In April 1957, Nagy was arrested in Romania and brought back to Hungary for a secret trial.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:37.450
The guns fell quiet, but the factories stood still. Nowhere did work resume.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:43.190
The workers' councils, elected in the factories since October, now led the resistance.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:51.810
On 14 November, they formed the Central Workers' Council of Greater Budapest, chaired by Sándor Rácz, aged 23.

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:57.210
They demanded the withdrawal of Soviet troops, free elections and Nagy's return.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:03.550
Kádár promised a great deal. But when they asked for it in writing, he said his word should be enough.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:12.000
On 21 November, Soviet tanks blocked a national meeting of the councils, and Budapest struck for 48 hours.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:18.100
On 4 December, several thousand women walked in silence to Heroes' Square with flowers.

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:24.390
Soviet soldiers were waiting, but India's ambassador came too, and the women laid their flowers.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:33.580
On 8 December in Salgótarján, a mining town, some 4,000 people demanded the release of two arrested council leaders.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:40.870
Security forces, police and Soviet soldiers fired into the crowd. How the shooting began is still disputed.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:45.860
46 of the dead are known by name, and estimates run higher.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.630
On 9 December, the government banned the Central Workers' Council.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:59.050
On 11 December, Kádár invited Rácz and the council's secretary, Sándor Bali, to Parliament, where they were arrested.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:07.650
The strike of 11 and 12 December was the last. By mid-December, out of sheer need, work resumed.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:17.140
Why did no help come? At the UN, the Soviet Union vetoed action in the Security Council.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:25.260
On 4 November, the General Assembly called on it to withdraw, by 50 votes to 8. Moscow and Kádár ignored it.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:32.950
In the same days, Israel, Britain and France attacked Egypt over the Suez Canal, and the United States opposed them.

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:38.920
The United States condemned the Soviet attack, but would not risk a world war over Hungary.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:48.060
On 27 October, Secretary of State Dulles had said, "We do not look upon these nations as potential military allies."

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:53.930
Radio Free Europe, broadcasting from Munich with American money, had encouraged the fighters.

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:59.450
Its own review found no direct promise of military help, with one exception.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:06.940
On 4 November, it quoted a London paper suggesting that American help might come if Hungarians held out.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:16.990
Many listeners heard a promise. In a survey of 1,000 refugees in Austria, 96 percent had trusted in some American help.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:23.590
The fighters held out for a week, the workers for five. By mid-December, the revolution had been crushed.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:30.260
By then, refugees were crossing the border, some 200,000 in all, and the reprisals had begun.

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:40.260
For over thirty years, the regime called 1956 a "counter-revolution". What it left behind is the next part of the story.
